Ladybird’s contentScripts.register() keeps each registration with the
context that made it, drops it when that context goes, and refuses a
match outside the extension’s host permissions with “Permission denied
to register a content script for <pattern>” — the message extensions
see, kept exact. That’s Firefox’s ext-contentScripts.js: register()
checks each pattern against extension.allowedOrigins, and the
context’s callOnClose() unregisters every script it registered. Its
argument errors follow Firefox’s Schemas.sys.mjs framing (“Type error
for parameter contentScriptOptions (...) for contentScripts.register.”).
Chromium and WebKit have only scripting.registerContentScripts(), with
no per-context lifetime.
